To satisfy the first condition, $P(x)$ must be always positive. However, this contradicts the requirement that all permutations have at least one real root. The key lies in the structure of the coefficients. If the coefficients are symmetrically arranged around zero, swapping any two coefficients would preserve the sign, but since they are positive, the resulting polynomial remains positive. This would mean no real roots. 

