Let $ P(x) = x^k Q(x) $, where $ Q(0) \neq 0 $. If $ n = p^k $ for a prime $ p $ not dividing $ k Q(1) + Q'(1) $, then $ P(n) \equiv 0 \mod n $, implying $ n \mid P(n) - P(0) $. The gcd of all differences $ P(n - i) - P(i) $ for $ i = 0, 1, \dots $ is $ n $, as $ n $ divides each difference. Since the gcd is 1, this implies $ n $ is a prime power, and there are infinitely many such $ n $ by choosing appropriate $ k $ and primes $ p $.
