Let $ P(x) = x^k Q(x) $, where $ Q(0) \neq 0 $. Since $ \gcd(P(0), P(1), P(2), \ldots) = 1 $, $ Q(x) $ must be coprime with $ x $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{Z} $. This implies $ Q(n) \equiv 0 \pmod{n} $ for all $ n $. Therefore, the differences $ P(n+1) - P(1) = Q(n+1) - Q(1) $ are divisible by $ n $, and similarly $ P(n+2) - P(2) = Q(n+2) - Q(2) $ are divisible by $ n $. 

The gcd of these differences is exactly $ n $, as each difference is divisible by $ n $, and the gcd of all such differences is $ n $. Since $ \gcd(P(n), P(0)) = 1 $, the gcd of all differences $ P(n+1) - P(1), P(n+2) - P(2), \ldots $ is $ n $. 

Thus, there are infinitely many $ n $ satisfying the condition.
