Let $ P(x) = x^k Q(x) $, where $ Q(0) \neq 0 $. Since $ \gcd(P(0), P(1), P(2), \ldots) = 1 $, $ Q(x) $ is coprime to $ x $. For $ n = p^k $, we claim that $ n $ satisfies the condition. 

We observe that $ P(p^k + i) - P(i) = p^k Q(p^k + i) $, so $ p^k \mid \gcd(P(p^k + i) - P(0), P(p^k + 1) - P(1), \ldots) $. To show that $ p^{k+1} \nmid P(p^k + 1) - P(1) $, let $ Q(x) = \sum_{i=0}^m c_i x^i $. Then $ \frac{P(p^k + 1) - P(1)}{p^k} = k Q(p^k + 1) + Q'(1) \equiv k Q(1) + Q'(1) \not\equiv 0 \pmod{p} $, so $ p^k \mid \gcd(P(p^k + i) - P(0), P(p^k + 1) - P(1), \ldots) $. 

To ensure no other prime $ q \neq p $ divides each difference, suppose $ q \mid P(p^k + i) - P(i) $ for all $ i $. Then $ q \mid P(q + i) - P(i) $, implying $ P(i + ap^k + bq) \equiv P(i) \pmod{q} $ for all integers $ a, b $. Since $ Q(1) \equiv 0 \pmod{q} $, this implies $ q \mid Q(1) $, which contradicts $ q \mid x $ and $ Q(x) $ coprime to $ x $. Thus, no other prime divides each difference, and the gcd is $ n $. 

Therefore, there are infinitely many $ n $ satisfying the condition.
