Let $ P(x) = x^k Q(x) $, where $ Q(0) \neq 0 $. We claim that if $ n = p^k $ for any prime $ p $ not dividing $ k Q(1) + Q'(1) $, then $ n $ satisfies the condition. Since $ P(0) = 0 $, we have $ P(p^k + i) - P(i) \equiv 0 \pmod{p^k} $ for all $ i $, implying $ p^k \mid \gcd(P(p^k + i) - P(0), P(p^k + 1) - P(1), \dots) $. 

To show that no other prime $ q \neq p $ divides each term, assume for contradiction that $ q \mid P(p^k + i) - P(i) $ for all $ i $. Since $ \gcd(p^k, q) = 1 $, we can choose $ a, b $ such that $ ap^k + bq = 1 $, leading to $ q \mid P(i + 1) - P(i) $ for all $ i $. This contradiction implies that $ q \mid \gcd(P(p^k + i) - P(0), \dots) $, hence $ q \nmid \gcd(P(p^k + i) - P(0), \dots) $. 

Thus, for each prime $ p $, there are infinitely many $ n = p^k $ satisfying the condition. Since there are infinitely many primes, there are infinitely many $ n $ such that $ \gcd(P(n) - P(0), \dots) = n $.
