Let $ P(x) = x^k Q(x) $, where $ Q(0) \neq 0 $. If $ n = p^k $ for any prime $ p $ not dividing $ k Q(1) + Q'(1) $, then $ p^k \mid P(p^k + i) - P(i) $ for all $ i $, so $ p^k \mid \gcd(P(p^k + i) - P(0), P(p^k + 1) - P(1), \dots) $. To show that $ p^{k+1} \nmid P(p^k + 1) - P(1) $, consider $ Q(x) = \sum_{i=0}^m c_i x^i $. Then $ \frac{P(p^k + 1) - P(1)}{p^k} = k Q(p^k + 1) + Q'(1) \equiv k Q(1) + Q'(1) \not\equiv 0 \pmod{p} $, so $ p^k $ divides the gcd. Now assume for contradiction that $ q \mid P(p^k + i) - P(i) $ for all $ i $. Since $ q \mid P(q + i) - P(i) $, we have $ P(i + ap^k + bq) \equiv P(i) \pmod{q} $ for all integers $ a, b $. Choose $ a = 1, b = 1 $, so $ q \mid P(i + 1) - P(i) $, but $ q \mid P(0) = 0 $, leading to $ q \mid P(i) $ for all $ i $, contradicting the initial gcd condition. Thus, there are infinitely many $ n $ such that $ \gcd(P(n) - P(0), P(n+1) - P(1), \dots) = n $.
