The minimal sum of distances from any set of 120 points on the boundary of a unit square to a point $ Q $ on the same boundary is achieved when $ Q $ is the center of the square. The distance from the center to each side is $ \frac{1}{2} $, and since there are 120 points, the total sum is $ 120 \times \frac{1}{2} = 60 $. This result holds for any configuration of points on the boundary, as the problem guarantees the existence of such a point $ Q $ for any set.
