The set $ \mathcal{U} $ of 120 points on the unit square's boundary forms a closed interval $ I_{\mathcal{U}} $ under the function $ g_{\mathcal{U}}(Q) = \sum_{i=1}^{120} |QP_i| $. By Lemma 2, this interval must intersect with any other set $ \mathcal{U}' $, implying that the distances $ g_{\mathcal{U}}(Q) $ must span an interval $ [a, b] $, where $ a $ and $ b $ are the minimal and maximal possible values of the sum $ g_{\mathcal{U}}(Q) $. 

Since the sum $ g_{\mathcal{U}}(Q) $ is continuous and bounded, the average value $ t $ of the interval $ [a, b] $ must satisfy $ t = \frac{a + b}{2} $ for any configuration of points. This ensures that no matter where the points are placed on the square, there will always exist a point $ Q $ such that the sum of distances equals $ t $. 

