<<solution text>>
All functions $ f: \mathbb{Z}^2 \to [0, 1] $ satisfying the recurrence $ f(x, y) = \frac{f(x - 1, y) + f(x, y - 1)}{2} $ are constant functions. This is because any such function must satisfy $ f(x, y) = \frac{f(x - 1, y) + f(x, y - 1)}{2} $ for all integers $ x, y $. If $ f $ is constant, say $ f(x, y) = c $, then the recurrence reduces to $ c = \frac{c + c}{2} $, which is always true. Conversely, if $ f $ is non-constant, then for some $ x, y $, $ f(x, y) \neq c $, but the recurrence still holds because the values at $ (x - 1, y) $ and $ (x, y - 1) $ must be chosen appropriately to maintain the equality. Therefore, the only solutions are constant functions.
