The locus of points $ P $ is the set of points where the angles $ \angle PAB + \angle PBC + \angle PCA = 90^\circ $ and $ \angle PAB = \angle PAC $, $ \angle PBC = \angle PBA $, $ \angle PCA = \angle PCB $ are satisfied. These conditions are equivalent to $ \{x_1, x_2, x_3\} = \{y_1, y_2, y_3\} $, where $ x_i = \angle PAB $, $ y_i = \angle PAC $, and $ x_i = y_i $ for each angle. Since all six angles are in $ (0, \pi/2) $, the equality holds only if $ \tan x_1 \tan x_2 + \tan x_2 \tan x_3 + \tan x_3 \tan x_1 = \tan y_1 \tan y_2 + \tan y_2 \tan y_3 + \tan y_3 \tan y_1 $, which is true only for the incenter, circumcenter, and orthocenter of triangle $ ABC $.
