The problem is disprovable. For any fixed positive integer $ k $ and positive reals $ \alpha, \beta $, there does not exist a positive integer $ n > 1 $ such that $ \frac{\omega(n+k)}{\omega(n)} > \alpha $ and $ \frac{\Omega(n+k)}{\Omega(n)} < \beta $. 

The partial solution demonstrates that the sum $ \sum_{p \le x} \omega(p+k) $ is bounded by $ \log \log x + O\left(\frac{x}{\log x}\right) $, which implies that $ \frac{\omega(n+k)}{\omega(n)} $ tends to 0 as $ n \to \infty $. This contradicts the requirement for $ \frac{\omega(n+k)}{\omega(n)} > \alpha $, and similarly, the sum $ \Omega(n+k) $ grows slower than $ \Omega(n) $, making $ \frac{\Omega(n+k)}{\Omega(n)} < \beta $ impossible. Thus, the existence of such an $ n $ is impossible under the given conditions.
