The problem asks whether there exists a positive integer $ k $ such that there are exactly 2019 $ k $-good positive integers. A $ k $-good positive integer is a number $ a $ that can be expressed as a good sequence where $ a_k = a $. A good sequence satisfies the conditions that for any distinct $ m $ and $ n $, $ \gcd(m, n) \mid a_m^2 + a_n^2 $ and $ \gcd(a_m, a_n) \mid m^2 + n^2 $. This leads to the constraints that for any prime $ p $ and index $ n $, the exponent $ \nu_p(a_n) $ satisfies $ \nu_p(a_n) \le 2\nu_p(n) $. 

The number of $ k $-good positive integers is determined by the product over all primes $ p $ of $ (2\nu_p(i) - \left\lfloor \frac{\nu_p(i)}{2} \right\rfloor) $, where $ \nu_p(i) $ is the exponent of $ p $ in the prime factorization of $ i $. It is shown that this product is never divisible by 3, and thus cannot equal 2019. Therefore, there does not exist a $ k $ such that there are exactly 2019 $ k $-good positive integers.  

