Let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of arc $\widehat{BAC}$ and $\widehat{BC}$, respectively. Since $M$ and $N$ lie on the circumcircle and are midpoints of arcs, they are equidistant from all sides and lie on the perpendicular bisectors of $AB$, $AC$, and $BC$. The midpoints of arcs are also the centers of the circle, so $M$ and $N$ lie on the circumcircle. 

Point $D$ lies on the circumcircle such that $AD \parallel BC$. Since $AD$ is parallel to $BC$, triangle $ABD$ is similar to triangle $ABC$. The tangency point $E$ of the $A$-excircle lies on the angle bisector of $\angle BAC$ and is equidistant from $AB$ and $AC$. 

Point $F$ is in $\triangle ABC$ such that $FI \parallel BC$ and $\angle BAF = \angle EAC$. This implies that $F$ lies on the angle bisector of $\angle BAC$, and since $FI \parallel BC$, triangle $FIB$ is similar to triangle $ABC$. 

Extending $NF$ to meet $\odot O$ at $G$ and $AG$ to meet $IF$ at $L$, we have $G$ and $L$ lying on the circumcircle. Line $AF$ and $DI$ intersect at $K$. 

Using the properties of cyclic quadrilaterals and the midpoints of arcs, we can show that $ML \perp NK$ by leveraging the symmetry of the problem and the parallelism of lines $AD$ and $FI$.
