Given distinct positive integers $ a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_{2020} $, the sequence $ a_n $ is defined as the smallest number not dividing the previous 2020 terms. The proof proceeds as follows:  

**Claim:** For sufficiently large $ n $, the least common multiple (LCM) of the first $ n $ terms satisfies $ \text{lcm}(a_1, \ldots, a_n) > n^{4040} $.  

**Claim:** The ratio $ \frac{a_n}{n} $ is bounded by a constant.  

**Proof:**  
Assume, for contradiction, that there exists a sufficiently large number $ k \gg c $ (where $ c $ is a constant) not in the sequence. Let $ k $ have a prime power factor $ p^\alpha \gg c $. Define $ T = \{1, 2, \ldots, a_t\} \setminus \{a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_t\} $, where $ t = a_t $. Since $ |T| \ge rt - t $, the LCM of $ T $ satisfies $ \text{lcm}(T) > ((r-1)t)^{4040} > (rt)^{2020} $. However, $ \text{lcm}(T) $ does not divide the product $ a_{t-2020} \cdots a_{t-1} $, which contradicts the definition of $ a_t $.  

