We aim to determine whether there exist two different sets $ A $ and $ B $, each consisting of at most $ 2011^2 $ positive integers, such that for every $ x $ with $ 0 < x < 1 $, the following inequality holds:
$$
\left| \sum_{a \in A} x^a - \sum_{b \in B} x^b \right| < (1 - x)^{2011}.
$$
Define $ P(x) = \left| \sum_{a \in A} x^a - \sum_{b \in B} x^b \right| $. We claim that such sets $ A $ and $ B $ exist by constructing $ A $ and $ B $ such that $ P(x) $ takes the form $ (1 - x)^{2011 + M} x^P $ for large integers $ M $ and $ P $. This implies that the first 2011 derivatives of $ P(x) $ at $ x = 1 $ are zero:
$$
P^{(i)}(1) = 0 \quad \text{for all} \quad 1 \leq i \leq 2011.
$$
To ensure such a form, we can construct $ A $ and $ B $ as sets where their generating functions differ by exactly $ (1 - x)^{2011} x^{2011} $. For instance, let $ A $ be the set of all positive integers up to $ 2011^2 $, and $ B $ be the set of all positive integers up to $ 2011^2 + 1 $. The difference $ P(x) $ would then be $ (1 - x)^{2011} x^{2011} $, satisfying the inequality for all $ x \in (0, 1) $. Thus, such sets $ A $ and $ B $ exist.
