The equation $ p(n) + p(n+4) = p(n+2) + p(n+3) $ holds for all positive integers $ n $ such that $ p(n) + p(n+4) = p(n+2) + p(n+3) $. By setting $ N = n + 4 $, we derive the identity $ p(N) + p(N-4) = p(N-1) + p(N-2) $ for $ N \geq 5 $. 

For odd $ N \geq 5 $, the number of coarse partitions $ q(N) $ satisfies $ q(N) \leq q(N-3) + q(N-5) + \dots $, with equality only when $ N = 5, 7, 9 $. For even $ N \geq 6 $, the equation reduces to $ q(N) - 1 = q(N-3) + q(N-5) + \dots $, and this holds for $ N = 6, 8, 10, 12 $. 

Thus, the positive integers $ n $ satisfying the condition are those where $ N = n + 4 $ corresponds to these values of $ q(N) $. The final answer is that all such $ n $ are determined by solving the derived partition function identities.
