All positive integers $ m $ except $ m = 1 $ satisfy the condition that there exists a prime number $ p $ such that $ n^m - m $ is not divisible by $ p $ for any integer $ n $.  
For $ m = 1 $, the expression becomes $ n - 1 $, which is not divisible by any prime $ p $ for $ n = 1 $ (since $ 1 - 1 = 0 $ is divisible by any prime), so $ m = 1 $ is valid.  
For $ m \geq 2 $, the expression $ n^m - m $ can be analyzed for divisibility by a prime $ p $. For example, choosing $ p = 2 $, $ n^2 - 2 $ is always odd, so it is not divisible by 2. Similarly, for $ m = 3 $, $ n^3 - 3 $ is congruent to $ n - 1 \mod 2 $, ensuring it is not divisible by 2 for all $ n $. Thus, all $ m \geq 2 $ satisfy the condition.  
The conclusion is that all positive integers except $ m = 1 $ are valid.
