The problem requires finding all positive integers $ m $ such that there exists a prime $ p $ where $ n^m - m $ is not divisible by $ p $ for any integer $ n $. We analyze the case $ m = 1 $ first: $ n - 1 $ is divisible by any prime $ p $, so $ m = 1 $ is valid. For $ m > 1 $, consider $ m = p^k l $ with $ \gcd(l, p) = 1 $. The expression $ (p^k l)^{p-1} + \cdots + 1 $ is not congruent to 1 modulo $ p^{k+1} $, so we choose a prime $ q $ not dividing $ m - 1 = p^k l - 1 $. This ensures $ \gcd(q, m - 1) = 1 $, leading to a contradiction. The key step is that $ m \not\equiv 1 \pmod{q} $, which contradicts $ m = 1 \mod q $, thus $ m \neq 1 $. Therefore, the only valid value of $ m $ is $ 1 $.
