The only function satisfying the given equation is $ f(x) = 0 $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $. 

By setting $ x = 0 $, we derive $ f(y^2) = f(0) + |y f(y)| $, which implies $ f(x + y^2) = f(x) + f(y^2) - f(0) $. Defining $ g(x) = f(x) - f(0) $, the equation becomes $ g(x + y^2) = g(x) + f(y^2) $, leading to $ g(x) = 0 $ for all $ x $. This confirms that the only solution is the zero function. 

Thus, the only function $ f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} $ satisfying the given condition is $ f(x) = 0 $ for all real numbers $ x $.
