The functional equation $ f(x + y^2) = f(x) + |y f(y)| $ implies that $ f $ is linear. By setting $ x = 0 $, we derive $ f(y^2) = f(0) + |y f(y)| $. Defining $ g(x) = f(x) - f(0) $, the equation becomes $ g(x + y) = g(x) + g(y) $, which is Cauchy's equation on $ \mathbb{Q}^+ $. The solution to this equation is $ g(x) = ax $, so $ f(x) = ax + f(0) $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $. 

If $ f(0) \neq 0 $, solving the equations $ a = |a + f(0)| $ and $ 4a = |4a + 2f(0)| $ leads to a contradiction, forcing $ f(0) = 0 $. Extending this to all real numbers, $ f(x) = ax $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $. The original equation is satisfied since $ f(x + y^2) = a(x + y^2) = f(x) + |y a y| $. 

Thus, all such functions are linear functions $ f(x) = ax $ for all $ x \in \mathbb{R} $.
