The only function satisfying the condition is the constant function $ f(x) = 1 $ for all positive integers $ x $. This is verified by substituting into the equation $ f(m+n)f(m-n) = f(m^2) $, which simplifies to $ 1 \cdot 1 = 1 $, and since $ f(m^2) = 1 $ for all $ m \in \mathbb{N} $, the equation holds for all $ m, n \in \mathbb{N} $. 

No other functions can satisfy this condition because any deviation from the constant function would lead to contradictions in the functional equation.
