All functions $ f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N} $ satisfying $ f(m+n)f(m-n) = f(m^2) $ for all $ m, n \in \mathbb{N} $ are constant functions equal to 1. This is because if $ f $ is constant, then $ f(m+n)f(m-n) = f(m^2) $ simplifies to $ f(m)^2 = f(m^2) $, which holds only if $ f(m) = 1 $ for all $ m $. Thus, the only solution is $ f(m) = 1 $.
