<<solution text>>
The number of distinct signatures modulo $ n $ is $ n^n $, as each signature corresponds to a unique sequence of residues $ f(1), f(2), \ldots, f(n) \mod n $. Since $ n $ is square-free (part a) or not divisible by a cube of a prime (part b), all $ n^n $ sequences are valid signatures of polynomials. Thus, the answer is $ n^n $.
