All positive integers $ n = 2^k \cdot p $ where $ k \geq 0 $ and $ p $ is an odd prime are solutions. 

The condition $ \sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = n $ is satisfied when $ f(n) = n $, which occurs if and only if $ f(n) \leq 0 $. For powers of 2, $ f(2^m) \leq -1 $, so $ 2^m \in S $. For odd primes $ p $, $ f(p) = 1 - 2p < 0 $, so $ p \in S $. The claim that $ 2^r p \notin S $ if and only if $ a + 1 \mid r $ (where $ a = \lfloor \log_2(p+1) \rfloor - 1 $) ensures that all such numbers satisfy $ f(n) = n $. 

Thus, all numbers of the form $ 2^k \cdot p $ with $ p $ odd prime and $ k \geq 0 $ are solutions.
