We first investigate when $ d(p + nq) $ is reducible, i.e., when there exist $ a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_\ell \in \mathbb{Z}^+ $ with $ \ell > 1 $ such that  
$$
d(p + nq) = \prod_{i=1}^\ell \left( d(p + a_i q) \right).
$$  
This implies  
$$
p + nq = d^{\ell - 1} \prod_{i=1}^\ell (p + a_i q),
$$  
where $ d(p + a_i q) $ are irreducible for all $ i $. Clearly, we must have $ d^{\ell - 1} \mid p + nq $.  

Reducing modulo $ q $ gives us $ p \equiv d^{\ell - 1} p^\ell \pmod{q} $, implying $ \gcd(q, d) = 1 $.  

Now, consider a reducible $ d(p + nq) \in S $ that can be factored as  
$$
d(p + nq) = \prod_{i=1}^\ell \left( d(p + a_i q) \right).
$$  
This implies  
$$
p + nq = d^{\ell - 1} \prod_{i=1}^\ell (p + a_i q),
$$  
where $ d(p + a_i q) $ are irreducible for all $ i $. Clearly, we must have $ d^{\ell - 1} \mid p + nq $.  

Since $ d $ is a factor of $ b $, and $ p + nq \equiv a \pmod{b} $, we conclude that $ p + nq $ is divisible by $ d $. Thus, $ p + nq = d \cdot k $ for some integer $ k $. This implies that the product of the irreducible factors includes at least one factor of $ d $, which means that the product has at least two factors of $ d $, and hence, the number of irreducible elements needed to express $ d(p + nq) $ is at least 2.  

Therefore, any element $ y \in S $ can be expressed as the product of at most $ t = 2 $
