For any $ n \geq 3 $, there exists a coloring of the sides and diagonals of a convex $ n $-gon such that every trio of colors can be used to form a triangle. This is because, in such a coloring, each triangle formed by three vertices will contain exactly one triangle of each color. Since there are $ \binom{n}{3} $ triangles, and each triangle uses three colors, the condition is satisfied for all $ n \geq 3 $. 

The conclusion is that the values of $ n $ for which such a coloring exists are all integers greater than or equal to 3.
