Let $ P $ be a convex $ n $-gon where each side and diagonal is colored with one of $ n $ distinct colors. We need to determine for which $ n $ there exists a coloring such that for any three of the $ n $ colors, there is always a triangle with those three colors.  

First, we observe that $ n $ cannot be even. This is because if any two distinct triangles get different color triples, there must be $ \binom{n-1}{2} $ triangles that get color $ i $, and each segment is part of exactly $ n-2 $ triangles, leading to $ \frac{n-1}{2} $ segments of color $ i $. This is an integer only if $ n $ is odd.  

For $ n = 3 $, a triangle is formed by three sides, each of which is a distinct color. Any three colors will form a triangle. For $ n = 4 $, a quadrilateral has only three sides, and any three colors will not form a triangle. Hence, $ n = 4 $ fails.  

For $ n = 5 $, a convex pentagon has five sides, and any three colors will form a triangle. This pattern holds for all $ n \geq 3 $, as each triangle uses three sides, and there are $ n $ sides. Thus, for any $ n \geq 3 $, such a coloring exists.  

The conclusion is that $ n = 3 $ is the only value satisfying the condition.
